    "poster_id": 1991,
    "post_rendered": "Given a real number <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/d/a/fdaf8c316d2703a7ece349762e3777d498257bd8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\lambda &gt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"43\" height=\"13\" >,</span> let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\"  width=\"14\" height=\"12\" > be a point on the arc <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/d/2edcdc0d73ef64ec441f60a3a8dccdbd10aa61a5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BAC$\"  width=\"42\" height=\"13\" > of the circumcircle of <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/d/c3df79a4384bc29acd37e14b0430773d09964ddd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\bigtriangleup ABC$\"  width=\"58\" height=\"13\" >.</span>  Extend <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/7/f874b39870586f0504f50a667f2bafedddf47904.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BP$\"  width=\"28\" height=\"12\" > and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/1/3/d13fd509f3062449aaea6e10e1040b1d585063f0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CP$\"  width=\"28\" height=\"12\" > to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/b/e2bbebb3bd73f1ae5c64098ab0244f739abf7ca4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$U$\"  width=\"14\" height=\"12\" > and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/d/12d58aa29201da09d8e620f8698e3a37547f6b4a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$V$\"  width=\"14\" height=\"12\" > respectively such that <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/0/9/d09f358a8cb4777b4a9b68ebbfae9d41b4b86052.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BU = \\lambda BA$\"  width=\"92\" height=\"13\" >,</span> <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/c/bcc2c6b02acc60e7cf90e5e465de6c2b0602997c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CV = \\lambda CA$\"  width=\"91\" height=\"13\" >.</span> Then extend <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/0/740503169e35d7fe098d2965ecc8916690d219dd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$UV$\"  width=\"28\" height=\"12\" > to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/6/9866e3a998d628ba0941eb4fea0666ac391d149a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"13\" height=\"16\" > such that <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/c/d3c3249b75c5c9b9690d4c21c3ba7b1ce8253a51.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$UQ = \\lambda UV$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"92\" height=\"16\" >.</span> Find the locus of point <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/6/9866e3a998d628ba0941eb4fea0666ac391d149a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"13\" height=\"16\" >.</span>",
    "post_canonical": "Given a real number $\\lambda > 1$, let $P$ be a point on the arc $BAC$ of the circumcircle of $\\bigtriangleup ABC$.  Extend $BP$ and $CP$ to $U$ and $V$ respectively such that $BU = \\lambda BA$, $CV = \\lambda CA$. Then extend $UV$ to $Q$ such that $UQ = \\lambda UV$. Find the locus of point $Q$.",
