    "post_id": 239179,
    "post_type": "forum",
    "topic_id": 38389,
    "poster_id": 1991,
    "post_rendered": "Prove that there exists <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/1/f21b49d4a1d07db0896300cd7c894fdbb50883a6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m \\in \\mathbb{N}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"50\" height=\"13\" > such that there exists an integral sequence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/5/a/15a99f632962e3c30c576d3eee36c41a3ee04882.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\lbrace a_n \\rbrace$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"35\" height=\"18\" > which satisfies:<br>\n<br>\n<br>\n<b>I.</b> <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/8/d/58d70f1b84604bd4fb19082d700cd8b6a0c5c83d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_0 = 1, a_1 = 337$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"127\" height=\"16\" >;</span><br>\n<br>\n<b>II.</b> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/8/3/a831792dd3c8144493e84f7c38ba99e255a4c27a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(a_{n + 1} a_{n - 1} - a_n^2) + \\frac{3}{4}(a_{n + 1} + a_{n - 1} - 2a_n) = m, \\forall$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"377\" height=\"38\" > <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/8/578cf6c5b8058f6f8e6369416da6dcaf73f8fc01.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n \\geq 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"43\" height=\"14\" >;</span><br>\n<br>\n<b>III. </b><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/8/b/b8b3930ebc3ddad083b5b4364e2c5f88638535f0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{6}(a_n + 1)(2a_n + 1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"148\" height=\"37\" > is a perfect square <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/3/6/536ba5a392431e74fec5a8db2257504c85fcc206.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\forall$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"10\" height=\"13\" > <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/8/578cf6c5b8058f6f8e6369416da6dcaf73f8fc01.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n \\geq 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"43\" height=\"14\" >.</span>",
    "post_canonical": "Prove that there exists $m \\in \\mathbb{N}$ such that there exists an integral sequence $\\lbrace a_n \\rbrace$ which satisfies:\r\n\r\n\r\n[b]I.[/b] $a_0 = 1, a_1 = 337$;\r\n\r\n[b]II.[/b] $(a_{n + 1} a_{n - 1} - a_n^2) + \\frac{3}{4}(a_{n + 1} + a_{n - 1} - 2a_n) = m, \\forall$ $n \\geq 1$;\r\n\r\n[b]III. [/b]$\\frac{1}{6}(a_n + 1)(2a_n + 1)$ is a perfect square $\\forall$ $n \\geq 1$.",
    "username": "orl",
    "post_number": 1,
