    "post_rendered": "Ok here we go, Use the following double counting to stablish some lemmas.<br>\n<br>\nCall <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/e/d/8ed417fc2fad9904404efe4ec38ac508d3d64618.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m_k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"23\" height=\"10\" > partial bound for the max number of subsets such that we cant find six <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/4/2744600189f7f1df31756f2022b2d5212ef1c767.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"18\" height=\"15\" > with the desired property.<br>\n<br>\nFirst, count the number of sets of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\"  width=\"8\" height=\"12\" > elements such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\"  width=\"8\" height=\"12\" > of them belong to one <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/4/2744600189f7f1df31756f2022b2d5212ef1c767.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"18\" height=\"15\" >,</span> using this for <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/f/f7f0f830b5b25ea929198068e407e8c9f62f56c4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=7$\"  width=\"44\" height=\"12\" >,</span> we get that for <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/f/f7f0f830b5b25ea929198068e407e8c9f62f56c4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=7$\"  width=\"44\" height=\"12\" >,</span> <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/4/f/e4f5f5eec2b7b7ee08ce5fdc83b6424adb81ffd8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5{\\binom{7}{6}}&gt; 2m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"95\" height=\"53\" >,</span> so we get  <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/1/1f1a1a0e864128efcc601f396f25cb66c388d28c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m_7=17$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"65\" height=\"14\" >.</span><br>\n<br>\nNow counting the number of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/a/e0a0db32027a732ac57d37ef2ae9bb150f65b108.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7$\"  width=\"9\" height=\"12\" > elements set, such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\"  width=\"8\" height=\"12\" > of them belong to an <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/4/2744600189f7f1df31756f2022b2d5212ef1c767.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"18\" height=\"15\" >,</span> for <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/d/4/1d4f171162b090bab7ce5dfa1c58715850530ba5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=8$\"  width=\"43\" height=\"12\" >,</span> we get <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/a/b6a3933ac14e8c2ce4418edfe432eec8cf92f384.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m_8=45$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"65\" height=\"15\" >,</span><br>\n<br>\nand Finally for the other <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\"  width=\"10\" height=\"8\" >,</span> counting the number of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/5/8455f3b5cb3b4880b8c9d782a5c1f0334db819eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8$\"  width=\"8\" height=\"12\" > element sets, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\"  width=\"8\" height=\"12\" > of them in an <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/4/2744600189f7f1df31756f2022b2d5212ef1c767.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"18\" height=\"15\" >,</span> we get a better bound that the desired one.",
    "post_canonical": "Ok here we go, Use the following double counting to stablish some lemmas.\r\n\r\nCall $m_k$ partial bound for the max number of subsets such that we cant find six $A_i$ with the desired property.\r\n\r\nFirst, count the number of sets of $6$ elements such that $5$ of them belong to one $A_i$, using this for $n=7$, we get that for $n=7$, $5{\\binom{7}{6}}> 2m$, so we get  $m_7=17$.\r\n\r\nNow counting the number of $7$ elements set, such that $5$ of them belong to an $A_i$, for $n=8$, we get $m_8=45$,\r\n\r\nand Finally for the other $n$, counting the number of $8$ element sets, $5$ of them in an $A_i$, we get a better bound that the desired one.",
